spec:
  grid: 23-30 rows x 28-30 cols
  background: single color (always 0 [observed]); top row and left col are entirely bg
  tile_lattice:
    layout: regular grid of framed tiles on bg, uniform square period 5-7
    interior_block: 1x1 to 3x3 solid square; frame thickness always 1
    bg_gap: period minus tile_size, range 1-3 (same in both axes)
    tile_grid_size: 4-5 rows x 4-6 cols of complete tiles
    margin: 1 bg row/col at top-left; grid may extend past last complete tile (showing partial next row/col of frame) or clip last tile by 1 row/col
    colors: 4-5 distinct colors total (bg, frame, default_interior, 1-2 marker colors)
  tiles:
    default: most tiles share one default interior color; count 8-22
    markers: 1-2 marker colors, each appearing in exactly 4 tiles forming axis-aligned rectangle corners on the tile grid
    marker_rectangle_span: 2-3 tile rows apart x 1-3 tile cols apart
    no_overlap: different marker colors must not share row or column bands, and their connector paths must not conflict
  output_rule: |
    For each marker color, paint its color into every bg cell along the 4 sides of the implied rectangle (two horizontal bands spanning between the two column bands, two vertical bands spanning between the two row bands). All original pixels preserved.

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick period P in [5,7], interior size S = P-3 or P-4 (so gap = P-S-2 in [1,3], frame=1). Tile rows R in [4,5], tile cols C in [4,6].
    2. Choose 4-5 distinct colors for bg, frame, default_interior, and 1-2 marker colors.
    3. Build the complete tile lattice (margin=1 bg, then R x C tiles at period P). Grid size = 1 + R*P +/- random trim in [-1, gap+1] for each axis (ensure all complete tile interiors are visible).
    4. Fill all tile interiors with default color.
    5. For each marker color, pick 2 distinct tile-row indices and 2 distinct tile-col indices (spans >= 1 each); ensure no row/col band overlap with other markers.
    6. Replace the 4 corner tile interiors with the marker color.
    7. Build output by painting marker color into bg cells along the 4 rectangle sides (horizontal and vertical bands between corners).
    8. Input = output with all painted connector cells reverted to bg.
```
